#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124329 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given 2x2 Matrix Is Diagonalizable"}] :question/id #uuid "f7b85641-289b-57b2-9ffc-ff4c5e10025a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8572" :question/problem "If the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nis diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D$." :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal.\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=3\n$$\n\nSince we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-3I$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - 3I &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} - 3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124330 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca18fa52-5494-53ad-bd02-208aba4ff9e1" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "854f39a0-99b3-59f3-a9d7-4909850254ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-8573" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP$, find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}$." :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=5$ and\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=-1\n$$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=-1\n$$\n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=-1\n$$\n\nComputing $A-(-1)I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - (-1)I &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} = R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1= -{2}x_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy setting $x_2=1$, we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\dfrac{-2}{1} = -2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124331 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "cb76e9dc-1195-58d3-8e58-a3421770265a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8574" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP$." :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=3, \\quad\\quad\\lambda_2=-2\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A$.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3$, we have\n$A - 3I|= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} - 3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$.\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n$M|= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} = R_{2} + (-\\frac{5}{8})R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). So, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_2=0$. Therefore, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nSetting $x_1=1$, we get the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- For $\\lambda_2=-2$, we have\n$A - (-2)I|= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} + 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nThe above matrix corresponds to a homogeneous system of linear equations. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac{8}{5}x_2$. Therefore, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{8}{5}x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nSetting $x_2=5$, we get the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124332 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "7959b6aa-7ef0-5ba1-b74b-5e59481d0145" :question/math-academy-id "e-8575" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ You're given that $\\lambda_1=4$ and $\\lambda_2=3$ are the eigenvalues of $A$, and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PDP^{-1} = A$, where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nwe construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P$, we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns:\n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4}$, and\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3}$.\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "866fad38-b391-5f47-826d-a124235c3b0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123041} {:answer/id #uuid "8816afb3-6e95-5510-83ae-fbd3f370272f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123042} {:answer/id #uuid "e264ccee-10f0-510d-b06b-f95df8c83c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123043} {:answer/id #uuid "5afa656a-e01f-533a-98f6-230d82d8d96f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123044} {:answer/id #uuid "5f61d9ce-ed43-547a-b6ad-6e54e1291cae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e264ccee-10f0-510d-b06b-f95df8c83c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123043} :answer-field/id #uuid "639ca4f3-b16f-529a-ba19-4c6d81d03d8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123040}] :question/id #uuid "1a91b641-268c-57ee-aae6-245277497fff" :question/math-academy-id "q-37600" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$. You're given that $λ_{1} =-3$ and $λ_{2} = 1$ are the eigenvalues of $A$, and\n\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$, $v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PDP^{-1} = A$, where $D$ is a diagonal matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given 2x2 Matrix Is Diagonalizable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "852e17c7-dbed-5fbc-a299-27637c36062e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123047} {:answer/id #uuid "d79d6192-cdd0-5422-a2d7-ff797d8838c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123048} {:answer/id #uuid "0d5de2b1-57ce-50c2-9134-a124c3881ade" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123049} {:answer/id #uuid "2f70813f-05d0-56ce-ae98-9dc7d6b0d9bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123050} {:answer/id #uuid "458bc46b-6ff5-5600-bb3d-4bcd5746d30a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f70813f-05d0-56ce-ae98-9dc7d6b0d9bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123050} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a3cc816-225c-582f-a7dd-93bff5cab380" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123046}] :question/id #uuid "fc9b7c52-0fe8-51bc-a93c-2e178718ab57" :question/math-academy-id "q-37609" :question/problem "If the matrix\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nis diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c1e0213-c23b-50ff-bd5f-e3e30c0fd289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a = 3$, $b = 1$, and $c =-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123059} {:answer/id #uuid "bddcee6d-a904-59fc-bc3b-324a49068c0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a = 1$, $b =-3$, and $c = 3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123060} {:answer/id #uuid "0f54a353-c1ec-5793-a065-fc448254ffaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a = 3$, $b =-1$, and $c = 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123061} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2159d2-1e8f-585f-b111-9720e155a611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a = 15$, $b =-1$, and $c =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123062} {:answer/id #uuid "59e4b9e4-9c0b-5a98-a7f7-8296593dd00b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a =-1$, $b = 3$, and $c =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f54a353-c1ec-5793-a065-fc448254ffaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a = 3$, $b =-1$, and $c = 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123061} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d0a1fa7-b4e5-5b48-a2f4-5e3d640098bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123058}] :question/id #uuid "52d32399-93d9-5ce5-a996-5d60c1e0900e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37610" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which $λ_{1} = 3$ and $λ_{2} = 15$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a, b, c$ given that\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & b \\\\ 2 & c\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & b \\\\ 2 & c\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}$?"}]}
